1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
The first human to get high on cannabis probably had no idea what cannabis was.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:12,000
They did not roll it. They did not name a strain. They did not spend 20 minutes explaining that this particular plant

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:19,000
made them more creative. They may have thrown weeds onto a fire. Smoke entered the shelter. Someone's sense of time

4
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:26,000
quietly resigned. Food became unusually important. And one extremely alert ancestor began wondering whether

5
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:30,000
everyone else had always been breathing that loudly.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:38,000
That scene is possible. It is also impossible to prove. Cannabis does not arrive in archaeology with a label saying used deliberately for

7
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:47,000
intoxication. Seeds can mean food. Stems can mean rope. Pollen can come from a wild plant. A leaf inside a grave might

8
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:54,000
be medicine, ritual equipment, an offering, or simply a leaf that achieved excellent preservation.

9
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:03,000
So asking when humans first discovered cannabis creates a surprisingly difficult problem. Which discovery? The discovery that the plant existed, that

10
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:11,000
its stem made strong fiber, that its seeds could be eaten, that it could ease certain symptoms, or that heating the

11
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:17,000
resinrich flowers could alter consciousness and make a funeral considerably less ordinary.

12
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:25,000
These discoveries may have happened thousands of years apart. They may also have happened repeatedly in different communities with different kinds of

13
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:32,000
cannabis. The plant was useful long before the evidence says anyone deliberately got high from it. And the

14
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:40,000
strongest proof of psychoactive use does not come from a palace, a famous emperor, or an ancient medical book. It

15
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:49,000
comes from blackened wooden bowls in a cemetery high in the mountains. But to understand why those bowls matter, we have to begin when cannabis was merely a

16
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:56,000
weed. Cannabis evolved in Asia, although its precise wild birthplace is difficult to reconstruct because humans have

17
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:03,000
moved, cultivated, crossed, and abandoned it for millennia. Once a domesticated plant escapes a field and

18
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:12,000
grows on its own, it can look wild while carrying a deeply agricultural family history. The plant is an annual. It

19
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:20,000
grows quickly, tolerates disturbed ground, and often thrives in the exact environments humans create. river banks,

20
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:31,000
paths, animal camps, waste ground, and the edges of settlements. In other words, cannabis did not need to be discovered in a remote enchanted valley.

21
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:40,000
It may have volunteered beside the toilet area. Hunter gatherers moving through East or Central Asia would have encountered many plants. Most were

22
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:47,000
tested slowly through observation, accident, hunger, imitation, and generations of knowledge.

23
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:55,000
Cannabis offered several features worth noticing. Its seeds were nutritious. Its stock contained long, vast fibers. Its

24
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:02,000
resin had a powerful smell. And some plants could affect the mind. But not every cannabis plant affects the mind

25
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:09,000
equally. Wild and fiber type plants generally contain much less of the compounds associated with intoxication

26
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:16,000
than modern drug varieties. The main intoxicating molecule is THC, but fresh

27
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:22,000
cannabis mostly carries its acidic precursor THCA.

28
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:29,000
Heat converts much of that into active THC through a process called decarboxilation.

29
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:37,000
Ancient humans did not know the chemistry. They only needed to notice that one plant did nothing, another made smoke smell unusual, and a third caused

30
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:46,000
the drummer to become emotionally involved with a rock. Even then, an accidental campfire may not have produced a dramatic effect. Open air

31
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:53,000
disperses smoke. Flames can destroy active compounds. Low potency plants mixed with ordinary fuel might cause

32
00:03:53,000 --> 00:04:05,000
nothing noticeable at all. Discovery probably required the right plant, enough reinous material, controlled heat, and an enclosed or semi-encclosed space. That is a demanding combination.

33
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:14,000
It explains why humans could live beside cannabis for centuries without inventing anything resembling intentional cannabis intoxication. Familiarity with a plant

34
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:19,000
does not mean knowledge of every use hidden inside it. Consider its stem.

35
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:28,000
When cannabis stocks are soaked or allowed to partially decay in a controlled process called reading, the softer tissues separate and release long

36
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:40,000
fibers from the outer stem. Those fibers can be cleaned, twisted, and made into cord, rope, nets, cloth, or other durable materials.

37
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:49,000
This may have been cannabis's first great attraction. A psychoactive experience lasts hours. A rope helps you carry tomorrow. Fiber leaves poor

38
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:57,000
evidence, however. It burns, rots, and disappears. Archaeologists sometimes find cord impressions pressed into

39
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:06,000
pottery, fragments of textiles, tools that may have processed fibers, or microscopic remains.

40
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:14,000
But identifying the original plant can be difficult. Some early claims that impressions must have come from hemp were later challenged because several

41
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:22,000
plants can produce similar fibers and patterns. Seeds survive better, especially when charred. Cannabis seeds

42
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:30,000
could be eaten whole, ground, or processed for oil. Yet, even a clearly identified seed does not reveal whether

43
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:40,000
people cultivated cannabis, gathered it wild, fed it to animals, used it as food, or valued another part of the same plant.

44
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:42,000
Archaeology keeps finding the object.

45
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:50,000
The intention has already left. Genetics adds another clue, but also another trap. A major genomic study proposed

46
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:57,000
that the ancestors of modern hemp and drug type cannabis separated from a basil domesticated population roughly

47
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:05,000
12,000 years ago in East Asia. That places early domestication around the dawn of agriculture. It is a remarkable

48
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:13,000
possibility. It is not a 12,000-year-old smoking session. Genetic dates are models built from living plant genomes,

49
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:21,000
mutation rates, sampling, and assumptions about population history. They estimate when lineages diverged.

50
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:29,000
They do not show what a particular human did with a particular plant on a particular evening. The earliest cultivators may have selected cannabis

51
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:37,000
for seeds, fiber, or both. A tall plant with long stems is useful for textiles.

52
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:44,000
A plant producing many large seeds is useful for food. Neither goal requires high THC.

53
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:49,000
In fact, selecting for fiber can pull the plant away from drug production.

54
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:56,000
Fiber crops are usually grown densely, so they rise tall with limited branching. Drug crops are often given

55
00:06:56,000 --> 00:07:03,000
space to branch and produce reinous female flowers. One species could therefore become several human

56
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:11,000
inventions. Hemp was not simply weak marijuana waiting for history to improve it. It was a crop shaped toward a

57
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:16,000
different job. The plant's reproductive biology made that shaping possible.

58
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:23,000
Cannabis often has separate male and female plants. Female flowers produce cannabonoid rich resin partly associated

59
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:30,000
with structures called glandular tricoms. If female plants remain unfertilized, they continue investing in

60
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:37,000
flowers and resin instead of seeds. At some point, people learned that different plants gave different effects.

61
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:45,000
Maybe they noticed that the sticky flowering tops were stronger than leaves. Maybe they favored plants with heavier aroma. Maybe they gathered from

62
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:50,000
wild populations naturally producing more THC.

63
00:07:50,000 --> 00:08:00,000
Maybe high elevation stress affected canabonoid production. Although exactly how environment, genetics, and ancient selection interacted remains uncertain.

64
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:08,000
Whatever happened, humans gradually separated the plant's personalities. One cannabis became rope, one became food,

65
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:15,000
one became medicine, and one began rearranging funerals. The first widely repeated stories about medical cannabis

66
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:25,000
often mention a legendary Chinese ruler called Shenong and give an impressively exact date around 2,700 B.CE.

67
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:34,000
According to later tradition, this divine farmer tested plants and taught medicine. It is a wonderful story. It is not a contemporary medical record from

68
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:37,000
2,700 B.CE.

69
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:46,000
The herbal work associated with Shaong was compiled much later, probably around the early centuries of the common era from traditions that may be older.

70
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:56,000
Treating the legendary date as if an imperial pharmacist signed and archived a cannabis prescription is historical enthusiasm getting ahead of evidence.

71
00:08:56,000 --> 00:09:07,000
Early Chinese texts do show that hemp became culturally important. Cannabis appears as a crop, a source of fiber, and a seed food.

72
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:16,000
Later pharmacological traditions discuss different plant parts and effects, including potentially harmful or vision producing consequences of excessive use.

73
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:23,000
But written medicine introduces the same ambiguity as archaeology. By the way, if you want to go even deeper into the

74
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:32,000
secret lives of ancient humans, I just launched a full course called The Secret Lives of Ancient Humans. 10 modules, 3

75
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:41,000
plus hours of content covering everything we never talk about on YouTube, what they feared, how they communicated, what hunted them, and much

76
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:49,000
more. Plus, an exclusive PDF guide included. Link in the description. See you inside. The Chinese term usually

77
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:57,000
translated as hemp did not always specify a high THC drug plant. A preparation using seeds might have no

78
00:09:57,000 --> 00:10:05,000
intoxicating purpose. A claim about flowers, resin, or strange perceptions is more suggestive. Yet, surviving texts

79
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:12,000
were copied, edited, and interpreted across centuries. The first discovery of cannabis did not wait for writing.

80
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:17,000
Writing only entered the room much later and began taking credit.

81
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:26,000
More direct evidence appears in the dry burial grounds of Shing Jang in Amasi, northwestern China. At the Yanghai tombs

82
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:34,000
near Turpin, archaeologists found an extraordinary casia of cannabis associated with a man buried about 2,700

83
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:43,000
years ago. A leather basket and wooden bowl contained leaves, shoots, fruits, and flowering material. The desert

84
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:51,000
preserved the plant so well that researchers could examine its physical features, chemistry, and genetic material. This was not a few stray seeds

85
00:10:51,000 --> 00:11:00,000
blown into a grave. It was roughly 789 g of prepared plant material. The cannabis showed characteristics consistent with a

86
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:09,000
variety valued for psychoactive properties rather than fiber alone. The larger stems and male plant parts had been removed. Resin producing structures

87
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:17,000
remained. Researchers found evidence that the plant originally contained THC related compounds, although thousands of

88
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:26,000
years of oxidation and decomposition changed the chemistry. The man had been buried with writing equipment, weapons, musical instruments, and other goods.

89
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:33,000
Researchers suggested he may have held a ritual role and that the cannabis could have been used medically or psychoactively.

90
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:41,000
Could have been. The grave proves that people deliberately collected and valued potent cannabis. It does not prove how

91
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:48,000
they consumed it. No pipe was found. No smoking device sat conveniently beside an instruction manual. The plant could

92
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:57,000
have been burned, inhaled, eaten in a preparation, applied medically, or carried as ritual equipment. The Yanghai

93
00:11:57,000 --> 00:12:05,000
discovery moves us extremely close to intentional drug use. Then another cemetery supplies the smoke. Around 500

94
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:13,000
B.CE, CE, people buried their dead at Jerzangal Cemetery on the Pomeir Plateau in what is now western China. The site

95
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:22,000
stands about 3,000 m above sea level, surrounded by a harsh landscape that also sat near routes connecting communities across central Asia.

96
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:32,000
Inside several tombs, archaeologists found wooden brazers. They looked like small bowls with burned interiors. Some held stones that had been heated intensely.

97
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:41,000
Researchers analyzed the invisible chemical residues absorbed into the charred wood and stones using gas chromatography and mass spectrometry.

98
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:49,000
The instruments found cannabonol canabonol or CBN can form as THC

99
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:57,000
degrades through oxidation. Its presence along with the residue pattern indicated that cannabis with relatively high levels of psychoactive compounds had

100
00:12:57,000 --> 00:13:02,000
been burned in those brazers. This was not evidence from one suspicious bowl.

101
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:12,000
Cannabis markers appeared on nine of 10 brazers recovered from eight tombs. Now the scene becomes clearer. People placed hot stones inside wooden containers.

102
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:19,000
They added cannabis. The plant smoldered rather than disappearing instantly into flame. Participants inhaled the smoke

103
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:27,000
during mortuary ceremonies, probably inside an enclosed space or tent. No individual pipe was required. The room

104
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:34,000
was the pipe. This is among the earliest securely dated chemically verified evidence for people deliberately burning

105
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:41,000
cannabis in a ritual context about 2,500 years ago. And the location matters.

106
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:45,000
Jerzang lay near the crossroads of ancient movement through the pomears.

107
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:54,000
People, animals, crops, metals, music, technologies, and beliefs traveled through these mountain corridors. Some

108
00:13:54,000 --> 00:14:01,000
individuals buried at the cemetery had grown up elsewhere according to isotope evidence. Cannabis knowledge may have

109
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:06,000
moved through the same networks. Perhaps travelers exchanged potent plants.

110
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:13,000
Perhaps ritual specialists shared methods. Perhaps communities repeatedly encountered naturally strong cannabis at

111
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:20,000
high elevations and developed local traditions. The evidence cannot tell us whether the Jerzonal plants were

112
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:27,000
cultivated for THC or gathered from unusually psychoactive wild populations.

113
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:38,000
Researchers noted that mountain stress might encourage higher canabonoid production, but deliberate human selection is also possible. Either way, someone knew which cannabis to choose.

114
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:47,000
You do not prepare multiple brazers in multiple tombs by accidentally confusing funeral herbs with garden waste nine separate times. This was knowledge. It

115
00:14:47,000 --> 00:14:56,000
had a procedure. It had a setting. And it had meaning. Why use cannabis at a funeral? Altered states can change how

116
00:14:56,000 --> 00:15:04,000
people experience grief, music, memory, fear, and the presence of the dead.

117
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:13,000
Smoke transforms a space physically. It smells distinctive, obscures vision, irritates the eyes, changes breathing,

118
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:17,000
and creates a shared experience among participants.

119
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:25,000
The intoxication may have helped ritual specialists communicate with spirits, guide the dead, enter trance, relieve

120
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:31,000
anguish, or bind mourers together. We cannot know the exact belief system.

121
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:40,000
Chemical analysis can identify CBN. It cannot interview a ghost. But cannabis at Jerzankal was not recreational in the

122
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:48,000
modern sense. That does not mean nobody enjoyed it. Ritual and pleasure are perfectly capable of occupying the same

123
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:57,000
tent. It means the drug was embedded in a ceremony involving death, community, and possibly spiritual authority. At

124
00:15:57,000 --> 00:16:04,000
almost the same point in history, a Greek writer described something remarkably similar far to the west.

125
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:11,000
Herodotus writing in the fifth century B.C.E. claimed that the Cytheians placed hemp on red hot stones beneath felt

126
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:18,000
coverings. The plant produced dense vapor and the participants shouted with delight.

127
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:23,000
He interpreted the practice as a kind of cleansing bath used after burial.

128
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:33,000
Heroditus was an outsider and outsiders often explain unfamiliar rituals with the confidence of a man who has misunderstood half the conversation.

129
00:16:33,000 --> 00:16:44,000
Still, archaeology later found related equipment in Cythian contexts, including frames, coverings, vessels, stones, and cannabis seeds.

130
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:53,000
His report is not proof of the first use, but it shows that cannabis fumigation had become established across parts of the Eurasian step by the first

131
00:16:53,000 --> 00:17:03,000
millennium B.CE. The similarities do not necessarily mean one single cannabis religion stretched from the Pamirs to the Black Sea.

132
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:10,000
Mobile pastoralists lived inside vast exchange networks, however, and plants can travel with ideas. A seed weighs

133
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:17,000
almost nothing. A memorable evening travels even faster. Cannabis then appears in medical and ritual traditions

134
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:24,000
across wider regions, but the timelines become tangled. In South Asia, preparations associated with cannabis

135
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:28,000
developed deep cultural and religious importance.

136
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:37,000
Bahang made from cannabis leaves and other ingredients became one enduring form. Sanskrit terms in ancient texts

137
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:47,000
have sometimes been identified as cannabis, but not every identification is certain, and manuscripts often survive from much later than the traditions they describe.

138
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:55,000
In the ancient near east, scholars have proposed cannabis behind certain plant names in Assyrian and other records. The

139
00:17:55,000 --> 00:18:04,000
famous candidate is kunu, a term sometimes connected linguistically to cannabis. Maybe ancient plant names are

140
00:18:04,000 --> 00:18:14,000
treacherous. One word can shift between species, preparations, and regions. A resemblance between terms is suggestive, not a bag of chemically tested flowers.

141
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:18,000
Egypt attracts even bolder claims.

142
00:18:18,000 --> 00:18:26,000
Online histories regularly place cannabis inside fionic medicine, mummies, temple rituals, and nearly

143
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:34,000
every papyrus with an unreadable plant name. The evidence is much less cooperative. Some ancient Egyptian

144
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:41,000
prescriptions may refer to cannabis, but translations and botanical identifications remain debated. Claims

145
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:48,000
of canabonoid traces in mummies have faced serious questions about contamination methods and interpretation.

146
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:54,000
The past contains enough cannabis without quietly planting extra.

147
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:58,000
By the Greek and Roman periods, medical writers described hemp more clearly.

148
00:18:58,000 --> 00:19:09,000
Dioscarites in the first century CE discussed cultivated and wild cannabis, particularly seeds, and mentioned medicinal applications.

149
00:19:09,000 --> 00:19:17,000
Other writers connected heavy consumption with effects on sexuality or the head, sometimes accurately and sometimes with the fearless creativity

150
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:22,000
typical of ancient medicine. These texts show knowledge expanding beyond fiber.

151
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:30,000
Yet, they do not reveal who first discovered the effects. By the time a physician writes about a plant, generations of farmers, healers,

152
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:38,000
traders, ritual specialists, and patients have already experimented on themselves. The official record usually

153
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:42,000
begins after ordinary people have completed the dangerous research phase.

154
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:50,000
How then was psychoactive cannabis actually discovered? There are several plausible paths. The first is accidental

155
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:55,000
burning. Cannabis stocks may have been used as fuel after fiber processing.

156
00:19:55,000 --> 00:20:05,000
Wild plants could have entered fires with brush. Resin-rich flowers burned inside a poorly ventilated shelter might have produced an unmistakable effect.

157
00:20:05,000 --> 00:20:08,000
But one accident is not a tradition.

158
00:20:08,000 --> 00:20:15,000
Someone had to remember the correct plant part, season, dose, temperature, and setting. Someone had to repeat it.

159
00:20:15,000 --> 00:20:23,000
Someone had to convince others that the dizziness was spiritually meaningful rather than evidence that dinner had gone wrong.

160
00:20:23,000 --> 00:20:31,000
The second path is medicine. People testing plants for pain, sleep, inflammation, appetite, or other symptoms may have noticed mental

161
00:20:31,000 --> 00:20:40,000
effects. Cannabis taken orally behaves differently from inhaled cannabis. THC dissolves better in fat than water, and

162
00:20:40,000 --> 00:20:48,000
raw plant material is less intoxicating until heat converts THCA into THC.

163
00:20:48,000 --> 00:20:55,000
That means simply chewing fresh leaves might disappoint anyone expecting a revelation. Cooking reinous material

164
00:20:55,000 --> 00:21:02,000
with animal fat, oil, or milk could produce a stronger and longerlasting effect. But the delayed onset creates a

165
00:21:02,000 --> 00:21:12,000
classic human problem. Nothing has happened. I should take more. History is silent for the next several hours. The third path is deliberate selection.

166
00:21:12,000 --> 00:21:20,000
Farmers notice variation. One stock is taller, one seed is larger, one flower smells stronger. If a particular plant

167
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:27,000
became valuable for ritual, medicine, or pleasure, people could save its seeds and spread them. Over generations,

168
00:21:27,000 --> 00:21:34,000
selection would raise the traits humans wanted. This process probably occurred more than once. Cannabis does not divide

169
00:21:34,000 --> 00:21:43,000
neatly into an ancient hemp era followed by a sudden drug era. Different communities may have cultivated different populations for different

170
00:21:43,000 --> 00:21:50,000
purposes. Fiber, seed, and psychoactive use could overlap. The fourth path is

171
00:21:50,000 --> 00:21:58,000
contact between cultures. A community growing low, THC hemp, might meet traders carrying resinrich cannabis from

172
00:21:58,000 --> 00:22:05,000
another environment. The plant was familiar. The effect was not. This would feel less like discovering a species and

173
00:22:05,000 --> 00:22:13,000
more like discovering what somebody else had done to it. That distinction matters because the ancient world was connected.

174
00:22:13,000 --> 00:22:20,000
The Silk Roads were not one road and did not suddenly appear on an official opening day. Long before famous empires

175
00:22:20,000 --> 00:22:29,000
organized transcontinental trade, herders, farmers, miners, migrants, and marriage partners carried crops and

176
00:22:29,000 --> 00:22:35,000
customs across Eurasia. Cannabis could move east, west, south, and back again.

177
00:22:35,000 --> 00:22:40,000
So could methods of using it. Burn it on stones, place it beneath a covering.

178
00:22:40,000 --> 00:22:50,000
Choose the flowering tops. Mix it into a drink. Use it after death. Use it for pain. do not give that amount to the new

179
00:22:50,000 --> 00:22:56,000
apprentice. Again, over time, cannabis entered societies with very different moral systems. Some treated it as

180
00:22:56,000 --> 00:23:03,000
sacred, some medicinal, some ordinary, some suspicious.

181
00:23:03,000 --> 00:23:12,000
Islamic era physicians described cannabis preparations, while hashish gained visibility in the medieval Middle East. In parts of Africa, cannabis

182
00:23:12,000 --> 00:23:20,000
smoking developed through trade contacts and local innovation. European powers later moved hemp around the world for

183
00:23:20,000 --> 00:23:28,000
rope and sailcloth while psychoactive traditions traveled through colonial labor, migration, medicine, and

184
00:23:28,000 --> 00:23:35,000
underground markets. The same plant helped rig ships and alter consciousness. Few crops have maintained

185
00:23:35,000 --> 00:23:43,000
such an ambitious resume, but this later global story can obscure the original answer. When did humans first discover

186
00:23:43,000 --> 00:23:50,000
cannabis? almost certainly before history can name them. If discover means

187
00:23:50,000 --> 00:23:58,000
encountering the plant, humans in Asia may have known wild cannabis deep in prehistory. If it means domesticating

188
00:23:58,000 --> 00:24:06,000
cannabis, genomic research points toward East Asia around 12,000 years ago, although the estimate remains a model

